# Cursor Rules for Foundry Smart Contract Project

## Project Patterns

### Code Style

- Use 4-space indentation for Solidity files
- Follow NatSpec documentation style for all contracts and functions
- Version contracts explicitly in file names (e.g., `ContractName_v1.sol`)
- Separate interfaces from implementations with 'I' prefix

### Architectural Patterns

- Always define interfaces for contract functionality
- Use OpenZeppelin contracts for standard functionality when possible
- Implement proper access control for sensitive functions
- Use custom errors instead of revert strings for gas efficiency
- Create helper functions for repetitive code patterns
- Handle rounding errors explicitly in distribution mechanisms

### Development Workflow

- Write tests for all contract functionality
- Test on Anvil local node before deploying to testnets
- Verify contract code on Etherscan after deployment
- Keep foundry.toml updated with network configurations
- Update tests when modifying contract code, not the other way around

## File Organization

- `src/` - Contract source code
- `src/interfaces/` - Contract interfaces
- `test/` - Test files
- `script/` - Deployment scripts
- `lib/` - Dependencies

## Testing Standards

- Unit test each function individually
- Test edge cases and failure modes
- Use fuzz testing for parameter validation
- Simulate different network conditions when relevant
- Create specific tests for distribution ratios and rounding errors
- Verify complete token distribution with no remainders
- Check ratio preservation between package types

## Security Principles

- Always restrict access to sensitive functions
- Validate all inputs thoroughly
- Follow the checks-effects-interactions pattern
- Consider gas optimization without sacrificing readability
- Ensure no tokens can be left stranded in contracts
- Handle rounding errors explicitly rather than ignoring them
- Transfer any remaining tokens to admin as a safety mechanism

## Tool Usage

- Use `forge build` to compile contracts
- Use `forge test` to run tests
- Use `forge fmt` to format code
- Use `anvil` for local development
- Use `cast` for contract interaction

## Learnings

### Token Distribution

- Integer division in Solidity can result in small rounding errors (often just 1 wei)
- Always account for these rounding errors in distribution mechanisms
- Transfer any remaining tokens to a designated recipient (usually admin)
- Verify actual ratios match expected ratios in distribution calculations
- Use constructor parameters to set contribution requirements rather than hardcoding them
- Derive distribution ratios from contribution requirements for simplicity

### Code Organization

- Refactor repetitive code into helper functions
- Use separate functions for package statistics updates
- Create getter functions that can handle multiple return types
- Keep core business logic in main functions and move implementation details to helpers
- Prefer function arguments over storage reads when possible for gas efficiency

### Participation Handling

- Consolidate similar participation logic into internal functions
- Apply the same validation checks across all participation methods
- Use consistent error messages for similar validation failures
- Make contribution requirements configurable through constructor
- Verify minimum contribution requirements in a consistent way

### Memory Management

- Be aware of storage vs. memory usage for gas optimization
- Use memory for temporary calculations
- Store final results in storage only when necessary
- Pass structs by reference when possible to save gas
- Keep helper function gas costs in mind when refactoring
